WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.768
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.768 --> 00:00:04.771
you know from TV shows
such as Marvel's "Loki"

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:06.473
as well as HBO's
"Lovecraft Country."

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:08.040
He stars in
"The Harder They Fall,"

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.576
which is now streaming
on Netflix.

00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:10.943
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:13.479
-You here for your
damsel in distress?

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:19.786 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:26.059 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:30.230
-You don't look hard of hearing.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.665
So I'm gonna ask you again.

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:35.101
Where's Ruby's book?

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:38.638
-Well, clearly...
you don't know me.

00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:41.674
-Please welcome to the show
Jonathan Majors.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.076
How are you Jonathan?

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:45.344
-[ Laughs ] Hey. What's up?

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:48.981
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:50.483
Ooh.

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:52.217
-Now, I'm a little bummed
you're not here in person,

00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:54.654
but I am hopeful that
I'll bump into you next week.

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:57.156
You're going to be here
in the city, in this building

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:59.225
hosting "SNL."
Are you excited?

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.160
-Am I excited?

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.429
Uh, hell yeah, man, I am. I am.

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:05.732
And when I see you,
we're going to do a tutorial.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:07.467
And you're going to
do some tricks.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.235
You're going to show me
some things.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.070
-I'm going to show you
where all the bathrooms are,

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.605
and that's it.

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:14.140
-Okay.
-The rest -- It doesn't matter.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.708
You have to learn the rest
on your own.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.510
-Yeah, I'm looking
forward to it, man.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.312
It's -- It's wild, man.

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.847
I feel like it's gonna be
drama school on steroids.

00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:24.216
-I think that's a very good way
of looking at it.

00:01:24.216 --> 00:01:25.584
And I actually heard something,

00:01:25.584 --> 00:01:27.820
and I believe it started
for you in drama school --

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.855
that you keep hours

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:31.891
that might be very conducive
to the "SNL" schedule,

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.559
that you go to bed
at 10:00 p.m. at night

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:34.861
and wake up at 4:00 a.m.

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:36.829
Is this a thing
you actually still do?

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:40.700
-I do. I do.
As much as possible.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:41.934
-I hate you.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.469
[ Laughter ]
-It's tough.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:46.305
-Yeah.
-But it's helpful. It's helpful.

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:50.142
And when it gets crazy,
night shoots,

00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:52.245
you kind of just revert
back to drama school,

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:56.782
which is, like, class till 8:00,

00:01:56.782 --> 00:02:00.719
and then you start rehearsals
at like 10:00 at night

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:02.187
and then work through the night

00:02:02.187 --> 00:02:04.290
and then start back again
at 7:00 in the morning.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.792
So it's like that.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.127
Or you can go
to a newborn schedule,

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:09.462
which I don't know if you know.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.263
That's also hell.
-Yeah.

00:02:11.263 --> 00:02:14.033
-We'll be all right.
Then on top of that, jet lag.

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.469
So I'm looking forward to it.
-Yeah, that's right.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:17.503
You are going to be jetlagged

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:19.405
'cause you're in London
right now.

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:22.008
You know, we just had Kumail
here from a Marvel movie.

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:24.910
You are working on the new
"Ant-Man and the Wasp" film.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.012
And this is so nice.
You said you wanted to read off

00:02:27.012 --> 00:02:29.048
like five or six spoilers
for us.

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:30.249
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:32.518
-Yeah, so, first,
I want to say...

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:33.652
[ Mouthing words ]

00:02:33.652 --> 00:02:36.522
-[ Laughs ] Oh, n-- Ohh!

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:38.725
I hate when Zoom cuts out!

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:40.426
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.029
So you -- This Western
is an all-Black cast,

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:46.399
and it's a fictional story,
but you're playing real people.

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:48.134
-Yeah.
-Did you know anything

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:51.037
about Nat Love, the cowboy
you're playing in this film?

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:53.038
-I did not know much
about Nat Love.

00:02:53.038 --> 00:02:57.944
However, I'm from Texas,
so I knew a lot about cowboys.

00:02:57.944 --> 00:03:00.680
I knew a lot about the rodeo,
to a degree.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.981
But not much about Nat Love.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:05.083
He did write a book,
a 70-page autobiography,

00:03:05.083 --> 00:03:08.921
which essentially just talked
about how cool he was.

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:10.990
And so I said,
"Okay, I got that."

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.360
Let me just apply that to this
script and take it from there.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.162
But, no, I didn't know much
about him.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:17.964
-Did you -- Coming from Texas

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:20.166
and spending some time
on a farm,

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:22.067
did you know your way
on a horse?

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:24.770
Were you comfortable
horseback riding in the film?

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.707
-I -- Yeah. I would say --

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:30.510
So, I did grow up on a farm.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.978
I grew up also in Dallas --

00:03:32.978 --> 00:03:36.015
Duncanville, Texas.
For those who know, you know.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:39.552
But on this little farm
in Killeen, Texas.

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.253
And I would say --
Truth be told,

00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:42.922
I probably know more
about roosters...

00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:44.223
-Okay.

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:46.859
-...than I do about horses

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:51.664
when I started, you know,
doing the horse riding in films.

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:54.900
I had a run-in
with a rooster on a farm.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.470
-Okay.
-It's pretty -- I don't want --

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:01.740
I have to break it down.
Because roosters. Okay.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.379
So, on farms -- On my farm,
I would go out and get the eggs.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:09.615
And there was a particular
rooster on a particular day

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:12.085
in which I walked in, and
this rooster was not about it.

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:14.754
I don't know if you know much
about roosters, but they're

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:19.158
quite territorial
and they're quite aggressive.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:23.262
And unlike most animals,
roosters fight to the death.

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:25.365
-[ Laughter ]
-And so -- Yeah.

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:27.466
So, I go in to get the eggs
this one day,

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:29.636
and this particular rooster
on this particular day

00:04:29.636 --> 00:04:31.904
was not happy
with this particular boy.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:33.539
And so I go get the eggs,
and I come out.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:37.576
And the rooster is --
I'm about 9 years old,

00:04:37.576 --> 00:04:41.813
and this rooster is probably
the size of a Rottweiler,

00:04:41.813 --> 00:04:43.883
to me, at the moment.

00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:45.384
And he comes at me,

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:48.654
and me and this rooster stay in
this pen for about 12 minutes.

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:49.922
-[ Laughter ]
-And I leave.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:54.526
Yeah. I leave with my shins
all tore up.

00:04:54.526 --> 00:04:56.629
And I go into the house,

00:04:56.629 --> 00:05:00.632
and I come back with a water
hose, just to make it even.

00:05:00.632 --> 00:05:03.236
And me and this rooster
have it out some more.

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:07.339
And then my grandpa comes in
and grabs me and gets -- I g--

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:12.045
I get a butt-whupping all the
way from the pen to the house.

00:05:12.045 --> 00:05:14.846
As I learned about roosters,

00:05:14.846 --> 00:05:17.516
they're territorial and
they also don't like water.

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:20.386
Because if they get wet,
they can get sick.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.355
And that was our livelihood.

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:23.589
So they're territorial,

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:26.092
and they don't like to bathe,
apparently, so...

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:28.694
-When you were getting
your butt-whupping,

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:30.730
was the rooster watching

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:34.033
and saying, "You've played
right into my hands"?

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:36.035
-I bet he was, yeah.
-[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:38.770
-You know, one of the cool
things about this film

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:41.740
is there's a great
musical element.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.309
There's a lot of kind of songs

00:05:44.309 --> 00:05:46.545
from the kind of performers you
wouldn't expect in a Western.

00:05:46.545 --> 00:05:48.981
Jay-Z was a producer
on the film.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:50.849
And you got to sing
a little yourself.

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:53.519
Was that one of the things
that drew you to it?

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:57.923
-I mean, that's one thing
about creatives, right?

00:05:57.923 --> 00:05:59.592
I mean, you know this.

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:02.361
Like, you get us around each
other for a long enough time,

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:04.130
we're gonna try everything.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:07.300
You know, so you got the horse
riding, you got the acting.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.802
The music element was just --
just kind of came out of it,

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:14.339
you know, primarily because our
director, Jeymes Samuel, is --

00:06:14.339 --> 00:06:16.975
I mean, he comes from --
he's Seal's brother.

00:06:16.975 --> 00:06:21.213
He runs his own band.

00:06:21.213 --> 00:06:23.383
But, no, I didn't know it was
going to happen right away...

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:26.418
but really leaned into it
when it came.

00:06:26.418 --> 00:06:28.954
You know, with Jay,
you know, with Mr. Carter,

00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:30.856
you knew it was
gonna be musical.

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:32.258
Had no idea it was
gonna be that musical.

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:37.863
You got Jay-Z, you got Kid Cudi,
Ceelo, Laura Mvula.

00:06:37.863 --> 00:06:40.198
I mean, it's kind of crazy.
[ Chuckles ]

00:06:40.198 --> 00:06:42.968
-It's an amazing film,
and you're great in it.

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:45.304
And I really can't wait
for you to be here next week.

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:47.339
Thanks so much for making time
for us. We really appreciate it.

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:48.674
-Oh, my pleasure, man.

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.109
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:51.644
-Jonathan Majors, everybody.

00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:53.412
"The Harder They Fall"
is streaming on Netflix now.

00:06:53.412 --> 00:06:56.248
And next Saturday,
November 13th, don't miss "SNL"

00:06:56.248 --> 00:06:59.252
with host Jonathan Majors
and musical guest Taylor Swift!

